The starred normalization is the plethystic evaluation $F_{\mu}^*(x;q,t)=F_{\mu}[X/(1-t);q,t]$ multiplied by the standard Macdonald scalar $b_{\mu}(q,t)$. In the $q=1$ specialisation used for the CTMC we have

$$F_{\mu}^*(x;1,t)= rac{F_{\mu}(x;1,t)}{\prod_{i\ge 1}(t;t)_{m_i(\mu)}},\qquad (t;t)_m=\prod_{j=1}^m(1-t^j).$$

The factor depends only on the multiplicities $m_i(\mu)$ of each part size, so whenever $x_i>0$ and $0\le t<1$ the ratio is finite and positive. Consequently the stationary law

$$\pi(\mu)= rac{F_{\mu}^*(x;1,t)}{P_{\lambda}^*(x;1,t)}= rac{F_{\mu}(x;1,t)}{\displaystyle \sum_{
u\in S_n(\lambda)} F_{
u}(x;1,t) \prod_{i\ge 1}(t;t)_{m_i(
u)}^{-1}}\prod_{i\ge 1}(t;t)_{m_i(\mu)}^{-1}$$

is just the unstarred weight multiplied by the local product coming from plethystic substitution. The local-to-global compatibility follows because the CTMC transition rates are expressed in terms of ratios $F_{\mu}^*/F_{
u}^*$, and the common factors $(t;t)_{m_i}$ cancel except for the multiplicities that actually change, matching the combinatorial updates of the clock process. Thus the star vs non-star bridge is completely explicit: the starred law agrees with the unstarred one after dividing by the hook-content-type product, so the global sector sum remains a true probability normalization.
